{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit transfer duties and withholding penalties","requirement":"When a rental property is sold, the new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit and accrued interest and is responsible for investing it, giving notices, paying interest and returning it; willfully withholding an assisted tenant's deposit carries a $500 to $2,000 civil penalty per offense.","key_value":"$500-$2,000 per offense","coverage_conditions":"All New Jersey residential tenancies; the civil penalty applies where the tenant receives State or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense, collected and enforced by summary proceeding; unlawfully using deposit monies is a disorderly persons offense with a fine of at least $200 or up to 30 days' imprisonment, or both.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.1","quoted_span":"Any landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit made by or on behalf of a tenant who has received financial assistance through any Stat e or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance , may be penalized. The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination source-of-lawful-income protection","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not refuse to rent or discriminate in the rental of housing because of a person's source of lawful income, along with the other classes the LAD lists, and must treat renters equally.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships in New Jersey, except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and single-sex or age-restricted housing as to familial status.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Excludes two-family owner-occupied dwellings and rooms in owner- or resident-occupied single homes"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner or resident-occupied single home, and residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex (e.g. YMCA and age-restricted housing) as it pertains to familial status.","penalty":"Landlords who violate the LAD are subject to fines and penalties of up to $10,000 for a first offense, and may have to pay monetary damages and rent the unit to the complainant.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"prohibits discrimination when selling or renting property and requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender  identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Credit report and rental application charges","requirement":"A landlord may charge the tenant for the cost of a credit report and may request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification; landlords may use credit reports and background checks in deciding whether to approve an applicant.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Prospective tenants screened by New Jersey landlords; the text states no cap on what a landlord may charge.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"NJ DCA Landlord-Tenant Information, Credit Checks and Background Checks","quoted_span":"The landlord is allowed to charge the tenant for the cost of the report. The landlord may also request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good-cause requirement","requirement":"A landlord may recover possession of a residential unit only by consent or through the legal eviction process, and must have good cause, proved in court, to evict or fail to renew a lease; a Notice to Quit is required except for nonpayment of rent.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Dwelling units used as residences in New Jersey; notice requirements run through the Anti-Eviction Act, N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1 to 61.12.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey rent control framework","requirement":"New Jersey has no state law that establishes, governs or controls rents; municipalities may adopt rent control or rent leveling ordinances enforced by locally created boards.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential rental units in municipalities that have adopted rent control or rent leveling ordinances; no statewide limit applies.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Only where a municipality has adopted a rent control or rent leveling ordinance"},"exemptions":"HUD-owned or HUD-subsidized developments, unsubsidized developments with HUD-insured mortgages HUD finds have economic problems, and NJHMFA-financed projects are not subject to municipal rent control ordinances.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"NJ DCA Landlord-Tenant Information, Rent Control/Rent Increases","quoted_span":"The State of New Jersey has no laws that establish, govern or control rents. Municipalities may pass an ordinance establishing rent control or rent leveling. Locally created boards enforce these ordinances.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law rent-control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","key_value":"30 years","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings in New Jersey municipalities with rent control ordinances; the exemption runs from completion of construction.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act rent increase procedures and unconscionability","requirement":"A landlord cannot raise rent mid-lease: the landlord must terminate the old lease and offer a new lease at the increased rent with proper written notice. Any increase must not be unconscionable and must comply with municipal rent ordinances.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies in New Jersey; increases must comply with any municipal ordinance governing rent increases.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot raise the rent mid-lease term. The old lease must be terminated, and the new lease must have the increased rental payment. The landlord has to offer the tenant a new lease with the increased rent once the old lease has been terminated.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Conversion cost rent increase prohibition","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium or cooperative ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"Rents may still be increased to cover the cost of new services or amenities.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cos ts resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
